not recommended. For details, see [GOTO statement in
SQL
procedures at ibm.com](https://www.ibm.com/docs/no/db2/11.5?topic=procedures-goto-statement-in-sql).

Instead of jumping back to a previous statement using GOTO, consider using a loop.

Instead of exiting the WHILE loop with GOTO, consider using other control-of-flow statements (for example, RETURN or BREAK).

Example (Oracle):

    CREATE PROCEDURE test(n INT) AS
    DECLARE
        x INT;
    BEGIN
        x := 0;
        GOTO a;
        <<a>> x := 1;
        IF (n = 0) THEN
            GOTO a;
        END IF;
        WHILE TRUE
            LOOP
                GOTO b;
            END LOOP;
        <<b>> x := 3;
    END;

